  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot temperatures, high humidity, and severe \nthunderstorm chances look to continue through Friday. \n\nThe severe weather threat continues to decrease this evening as the \nthe remnant outflow/backdoor front washes out over the region. A \nleftover shower or two has been noted across up across \nWashington/Frederick counties, but this activity has gradually \ndecreased in coverage given the stability in the lower levels. \nScattered to broken mid and high level clouds will remain, \nespecially points east of I-81 given the washed out boundary nearby. \n\nOverall expecting a lull in any activity through at least 2-3am with \nthe potential for a secondary round storms dropping south from PA \nheading into early Thursday morning. This activity is currently \nlocated up across the Twin Tiers of New York/northeast PA where \nnumerous severe thunderstorm warnings are in effect. Hi-res guidance \npushes this activity slowly south into areas along and north of I-\n66/US-50 between 2am-6am Thursday morning. Guidance varies on \ncoverage with a focus across northern and central MD compared to \nlocations further south into Washington DC, southern MD, and \nnorthern VA. At this rate, not looking at any widespread severe \nweather with this batch of storms although locally gusty winds, \nheavy rainfall, and frequent lightning cannot be ruled out.\n\nThe big question is to how this round of morning convection (if it \noccurs) plays into what will happen in the afternoon. Current \nguidance suggest a scattering of storms originating in and around \nthe Alleghenies working east as another wave of energy pulses \nthrough. Some of guidance has limited activity east of the mountains \nwhile others have more. General consensus is for convection to pop \ntoward the mountains and west of US-15 between 11am-2pm before \nworking east into the metros between 3-9pm. Coverage remains \nuncertain given any convective debris from the morning. Even with \nthat said, expect some organization given 30-35 kts of shear aloft. \nDamaging winds will be the primary threat with storms given \nincreased DCAPE values on the order of 1000-1400 j/kg. Large hail \nalso cannot be ruled out due to decent lapse rates in the 6-7.5 \ndegree C/km range. As for flooding, an isolated instance or two \ncannot be ruled out, especially in urban areas or in the event a \ncell backbuilds/trains over a given area. Modeling continues to hold \nadditional thunderstorm threats Thursday night into Friday although \nconfidence in this is low pending the timing and placement of the \nincumbent cold frontal boundary. \n\nRight now, most of the guidance has a front dropping through the \narea either Friday morning into Friday afternoon or Friday afternoon \ninto Friday night. This would spell continue concerns for severe \nweather in locations along and south of I-70/I-68 where instability \nmay be maximized. The added lift from the front should help with \nbetter coverage and organization of t-storms Friday afternoon and \nevening. Damaging winds will once again be the main threat with \nstorms along with frequent lightning, locally heavy rainfall, and \nlarge hail as secondary concerns. \n\nStorms will have plenty of fuel to work with as highs pushing back \ninto the low to mid 90s Thursday afternoon. Locations south of I-\n66/US-50 and along/east of I-95 could see highs in the mid to upper \n90s. Heat indices will range from 100 to 105 degrees for locations \neast of the Blue Ridge. Heat Advisories have been hoisted for \nportions of northern and central VA, southern MD and the eastern WV \nPanhandle where criteria will be met. Baltimore/Washington DC Metros \nwill be just shy with heat indices likely hovering in the 100-104 \ndegree range. Temperatures will cool a bit Friday with highs in the \nupper 80s and low 90s. Heat indices will decrease due to the \nincumbent front, added convection, and increased cloud cover across \nthe region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Mostly dry conditions and lowering humidity for the \nLabor Day holiday weekend ahead. \n\nHigh pressure tracks southeast from the Great Lakes on Saturday, \npushing a cold front through region throughout the day. Some \nuncertainty still exists regarding how far south this front will \nadvance south before stalling, but most ensemble members depict this \nboundary south of our region by Saturday afternoon. This should \nbring a drop in heat & humidity across the Mid-Atlantic, with \nafternoon highs ranging in the mid-80s on Saturday. As mid-level dry \nair continues to track across the area, temperatures should continue \nto decrease as the weekend goes on. By the Labor Day holiday, it \nshould feel relatively mild & dry, with dewpoints ranging in the \nupper-50s across the region.",
